.\" scalaall(1) — comprehensive all-in-one man page for scalars
.\" Modeled after zshall(1): every man page in one document.
.\" Render with:  man -l man/man1/scalaall.1
.\" Install:      cp man/man1/scalaall.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/
.TH SCALAALL 1 "2026" "scalars 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scalaall \- the scalars comprehensive reference (invocation, language
slice, bytecode VM, Cranelift JIT, numeric semantics, editor/debugger
tooling)
.SH OVERVIEW
This is the all\-in\-one
.B scalars
manual, modeled on
.BR zshall (1).
It concatenates the major sections of the scalars reference into a single
page so you can grep with `/`:
.RS 2
.PP
.B 1.  INVOCATION
.RB "\(em " "file run + introspection + LSP/DAP flags"
.br
.B 2.  LANGUAGE SLICE
.RB "\(em " "entry object, statements, expressions, operators"
.br
.B 3.  VALUE MODEL
.RB "\(em " "Int/Double/Boolean/String/null on the fusevm value model"
.br
.B 4.  NUMERIC SEMANTICS
.RB "\(em " "Int/Double division dispatch, String +, structural ==, Double.toString"
.br
.B 5.  BYTECODE VM
.RB "\(em " "lowering to fusevm ops, no bespoke VM, no JVM"
.br
.B 6.  CRANELIFT JIT
.RB "\(em " "tiered compilation of hot loops"
.br
.B 7.  LSP SERVER
.RB "\(em " "completion, hover, live diagnostics"
.br
.B 8.  DAP ADAPTER
.RB "\(em " "source\-line breakpoints, stepping, variables"
.br
.B 9.  ENVIRONMENT
.br
.B 10. EXIT STATUS
.br
.B 11. FILES
.br
.B 12. EXAMPLES
.br
.B 13. GAPS
.RE
.PP
For a shorter quick\-reference page see
.BR scala (1).
.SH "1.  INVOCATION"
The program text is a single
.B .scala
file named as the first non\-option argument.  Arguments after the file
are collected but not yet exposed to the program (slice 1 ignores
.BR argv ).
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-tokens
Print the lexer token stream and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-ast
Print the parsed AST and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-disasm
Print the lowered fusevm bytecode disassembly and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-lsp
Speak the Language Server Protocol over stdio.
.TP
.B \-\-dap
Speak the Debug Adapter Protocol over stdio.
.TP
.BR \-version ", " \-\-version
Print the version banner and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the option grammar and exit.
.SH "2.  LANGUAGE SLICE"
.SS Entry object
A compilation unit is a single
.BR object .
scalars finds the entry point two ways:
.B object Name extends App { <body> }
runs
.B <body>
directly, and
.B object Name { ... def main(args: Array[String]): Unit = { <body> } ... }
runs its
.BR main .
Other members are skipped.  Optional
.BR package / import
prologue lines are tolerated and ignored.
.SS Statements
.BR val / var
bindings (with an optional
.BR ": Type"
annotation), assignment and compound assignment
.RB ( = ,
.BR += ,
.BR -= ,
.BR *= ,
.BR /= ,
.BR %= ),
.B if (cond) { .. } else { .. }
,
.B while (cond) { .. }
,
the range
.B for (name <- start until|to end) { .. }
comprehension in statement position, and
.BR println / print .
Statements are separated by inferred line breaks or explicit
.BR ; .
.SS Expressions
Integer, floating, string, char (as a one\-char string), boolean, and
.B null
literals; variable reads; unary
.BR - / ! ;
and the binary operators below with precedence climbing.
.SS Operators
.B + - * / %
(arithmetic),
.B == != < > <= >=
(comparison),
.B && ||
(short\-circuiting boolean).
.SS Newline inference
The hand\-written lexer emits a statement\-separator line break only where
the previous token can end a statement, the next can begin one, and the
cursor is not inside
.BR (...) / [...] .
.SH "3.  VALUE MODEL"
Slice\-1 scalars runs on the fusevm value model directly, with no object
heap of its own yet:
.BR Int
as a native integer,
.BR Double
as a float,
.BR Boolean
as a bool,
.BR String
as a VM string, and the
.B null
literal as
.BR Value::Undef .
Locals are addressed by name through
.BR GetVar / SetVar
in a single entry frame with no lexical scopes.
.SH "4.  NUMERIC SEMANTICS"
.SS Division
fusevm's native division always floats, but Scala's
.B /
truncates for two
.BR Int s
.RB ( "7 / 2 == 3" )
and floats when either operand is a
.B Double
.RB ( "7 / 2.0 == 3.5" ).
Because slice 1 carries no static types, the choice is made at runtime by
a type\-dispatching
.B SDIV
builtin.  Integer division by zero yields
.B null
(a documented gap; Scala throws
.BR ArithmeticException ).
.SS String +
Scala's
.B +
is string concatenation when a
.B String
operand is involved:
.B String.+(Any)
(a String left operand) and numeric
.B +(String)
(a numeric left operand, String right).  Other mixed
.B +
operands are rejected rather than silently concatenated.
.SS Structural ==
.B ==
/
.B !=
compare by value (Scala's
.B ==
is
.BR equals ),
so strings compare by content.
.SS Double.toString
Whole doubles print with a trailing
.B .0
; magnitude selects decimal notation for
.B 1e-3 <= |x| < 1e7
and computerized scientific
.RB ( 1.0E7 ,
.BR 1.0E-4 )
otherwise, reproducing Java's
.BR Double.toString .
Non\-finite values print
.BR NaN / Infinity / -Infinity .
.SH "5.  BYTECODE VM"
.B scalars
lowers the Scala AST to a flat
.B fusevm::Chunk
of stack\-VM ops
.RB ( LoadInt ,
.BR Add ,
.BR GetVar ,
.BR JumpIfFalse ,
.BR CallBuiltin ,
\(em) and runs it on the shared engine.  There is no local
.BR vm.rs / jit.rs
and no JVM.  Short\-circuit
.BR && / || ,
control flow, and the range
.B for
resolve to jump\-patched offsets at compile time; a
.B for
snapshots its upper bound into a synthetic local, matching Scala's
evaluate\-the\-range\-once semantics.
.SH "6.  CRANELIFT JIT"
Execution runs on fusevm's three\-tier Cranelift JIT; hot loops
trace\-compile to native code, and the
.B jit-disk-cache
feature persists compiled native code across runs keyed by chunk hash.
All\-numeric arithmetic stays on the native fast path; only operations
with a non\-numeric operand delegate to the strict numeric hook in the
host.
.SH "7.  LSP SERVER"
.B scala --lsp
speaks the Language Server Protocol over stdio.  It is self\-contained and
read\-only:
.IP \[bu] 2
.B completion
of the recognized keyword / contextual\-word / Predef / operator corpus;
.IP \[bu] 2
.B hover
rendering each name's category, one\-line doc, and a runnable example;
.IP \[bu] 2
.B diagnostics
computed by the runtime's own parser \(em a syntax error maps to a
diagnostic on the reported line.
.PP
The same corpus generates
.B docs/reference.html
via the
.B gen-docs
binary, so the editor and the static reference never drift.
.SH "8.  DAP ADAPTER"
.B scala --dap
speaks the Debug Adapter Protocol over stdio: a single\-threaded
source\-line debugger.  The program is compiled with a per\-statement
.B DBG_LINE
marker (emitted only in this mode) and run on the pure interpreter, so
breakpoints and stepping land on real lines.  Supported requests:
.BR setBreakpoints ,
.BR stackTrace ,
.BR scopes ,
.BR variables ,
.BR evaluate
(bare local lookup),
.BR continue ,
.BR next ,
.BR stepIn ,
.BR stepOut .
Program stdout is redirected to a pipe during the run and forwarded as
.B output
events, so
.BR println / print
never corrupt the JSON protocol channel.
.SH "9.  ENVIRONMENT"
scalars reads no environment variables of its own in the current slice.
The shared fusevm JIT / disk\-cache honor their own variables.
.SH "10. EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
Error during lexing, parsing, lowering, or execution.  Errors are written
to stderr as
.BR "scalars: <reason>" .
.SH "11. FILES"
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man1/scala.1
Short reference man page.
.TP
.I /usr/local/share/man/man1/scalaall.1
This man page.
.SH "12. EXAMPLES"
.PP
Run a Scala file:
.RS
.nf
scala hello.scala
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print the token stream, the AST, or the bytecode:
.RS
.nf
scala --dump-tokens hello.scala
scala --dump-ast   hello.scala
scala --disasm     hello.scala
.fi
.RE
.PP
An entry object with a range loop and Int/Double division:
.RS
.nf
object Demo extends App {
  var sum = 0
  for (i <- 1 to 10) { sum += i }
  println(sum)              // 55
  println(7 / 2)            // 3
  println(7 / 2.0)          // 3.5
  println("total = " + sum) // total = 55
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Editor / debugger tooling over stdio:
.RS
.nf
scala --lsp
scala --dap
.fi
.RE
.SH "13. GAPS"
.B scalars
is an early slice.  Documented gaps (see
.B BUGS.md
in the source tree):
.IP \[bu] 2
User\-defined methods, classes, traits, and the collection library are not
yet carried; a member call
.RB ( x.foo() )
or field access is a parse error.
.IP \[bu] 2
Integer division / modulo by zero yields
.B null
rather than throwing
.BR ArithmeticException .
.IP \[bu] 2
.BR val
reassignment is not rejected (no immutability enforcement yet).
.IP \[bu] 2
.B return ,
.B for ... yield ,
and the
.B by
range step are not yet supported.
